    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, my realtor just mentioned something about needing to set up an escrow account for my house purchase here in Toronto. I've never dealt with this before, can you explain what escrow actually is and why it's used during the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, escrow! It's like a well-guarded goblin vault, but for money and documents instead of gold. In real estate, escrow is a neutral third party that holds your deposit and the seller's paperwork until all the conditions of the sale are met. Think of it as a goblin banker who won't release the shiny coins until both sides have signed in blood—or, you know, signed the closing documents.\n\nIn Toronto, your realtor likely wants to protect your deposit. The escrow agent ensures the seller doesn't get your money until the title is clean and the keys are in your hand. It's a safety net, so neither side can pull a sneaky goblin trick and run off with the loot.",
      "source": "target",
